# `to(String, in:)` is supplied by a unit module, not the prelude. Without the
# `using`, the named argument matches no clause — say so, and name the module
# that does define one, rather than quietly formatting without it.
#
# KNOWN BEAM DIVERGENCE: BEAM rejects this at lowering with "named args to
# receiver function with unknown params: to". It catches the same mistake, but
# as a compile-time IR error with different wording and no `using` hint.
main do
  IO.printLine("before")
  IO.printLine((Units.SI.watt(11_111) * 1.hour).to(String, in: Units.SI.Kilo(Units.SI.Watt * Hour)))
end
